    "chunk_text": "1. One authoritative land system: At the core of the problem is a simple absence of hierarchy. Sri Lanka does not lack land institutions; it lacks a single system that all of them must ultimately answer to. The most basic reform principle is therefore this: there must be one authoritative land system. Special regimes can exist, but they cannot exist outside the land system. As long as planning authorities, investment bodies, infrastructure agencies, and reclamation authorities can independently alter or constrain land rights without reference to the registry, ownership certainty will remain conditional. A title can only mean what it says if no parallel authority can quietly rewrite it.\n\n3. Discipline exceptional powers with time and process: Finally, exceptional powers must be disciplined by time and process, not left to operate indefinitely. Many special regimes begin as temporary measures but evolve into permanent uncertainty. Restrictions should expire unless explicitly renewed, delays should trigger consequences rather than silence, and prolonged interventions should activate predictable compensation. Procedural tools such as time-bound decisions, deemed approvals, and parallel processing are not technical fixes; they are governance safeguards. Without them, delay remains the safest option and reform remains vulnerable to quiet resistance."
